5  during the coalition government  and is equally well known for his  foxtrot on a strictly  christmas special so um  i'm going to ask each of our uh  panelists to  lay out their case if you like um for  two minutes  uh before we get into the debate in a  slightly deeper fashion and the question  that i want each of them to respond to  is should we just accept that as  citizens we don't have the answers  and shouldn't we leave it to those who  are elected to find solutions to  intractable problems  john could i start with you certainly  well i find it encouraging when i think  i'm seeing at least some signs of  seeing the whole business at um the  necessary depth  of the problem the overarching uh  crisis and so we've seen uh  this is the way i would put it very  briefly civilizations come and go  they've risen they've fallen and  i would argue that there's now just one  civilization  a globalized uh totalizing one  under the sign of technology and capital  and it is visibly grandly failing  on every level in every sphere i think  and that's being born in on people not  just in terms of ideas but in terms of  life i think we're in the age of  pandemics where  the epidemics proliferate one after  another and tend to become  pandemics and of course that's where  we're at right now  uh the environmental catastrophe has  been arriving  unfolding the seas are rising they're  acidifying or warming they're full  full of plastics more and more mass  species  uh extinction the weather extremes the  global  air pollution on and on and on that's uh  a monstrous failure of civilization i  think  what drives all this and what if the  social of the soul  well we see a landscape a panorama of  anxiety depression  loneliness uh drug use  high suicide rates etc  uh you know you kind of look in vain to  find that the diamond order has any  answers at all  and so uh i think it's time to get  more serious if we're going to contend  with the  with the gravity of all this  and i think we've seen that uh one  particular part of it is  technology which i think is  to some degree come to fill the vacuum  left by the pretty broadly based  failure of political ideas political  claims and promises i think attract  relatively fewer people  that the massive advertising of  technology  that has the answers for everything it's  going to fix everything  uh you know that's that's certainly  ongoing  okay and it says we're all connected  we're more and more connected  well the fact is we're more and more  disconnected we're more and more  isolated  uh it claims that we're all empowered  via technology well we're more  disempowered so i'm guessing from  that that that you don't think there's  much point in leaving it to those who  are elected  to find solutions to this this landscape  that you  you've set out so we will come back to  these issues but right now i'd like to  go to aaron with the same question if i  may  in a way i'm going to repeat really a  lot of what what john has said  um and i think politics in many ways has  not been more important for for any of  our lives i think you can probably go  back to 1930s to say politics has such  significant for the outcomes of  individual  lives and that's because we're in an era  of crisis  and we talk repeatedly about an economic  crisis climate crisis  political representation so on it  becomes a cliche but i think  if you look at covid19 in particular  it's been described as a  a boomerang coming from the sixth great  extinction and hitting  the human race square and the jewel and  the reason being is that this is the  third novel coronavirus  we've seen this century curved 19 uh  other two are mers and sars and the  reason why we're seeing this accelerate  and there are around three thousand  distinct corona viruses amongst the  planet's bat population  is because of deforestation and because  of species displacement  effectively the whole world is now  adopting fast food and industrial  agriculture  and that means there are going to be as  john has just said there are going to be  many  many many more pandemics we should be  you know incredibly grateful this  doesn't have a fatality rate of  something like ebola  you know if you have a mix of something  like ebola with something which is 40  asymptomatic  you see tens hundreds of millions of  deaths now i'm not saying that to panic  anybody but  it's an important thought experiment  think how bad could this have been  for them for the next time and that's  just one aspect of the climate crisis  another is of course  as john said we've all got micro  particles of plastic in our bodies  uh we are looking at two degrees warming  this century which will mean  uh fresh drinking water for around a  third of the planet will disappear  then you've got attendant without the  economic crisis the background noise  tool of this since 2008 we've not seen  rising living standards now  we'll have some disagreement about that  vince will say well that can be  addressed with a counter cyclical  keynesian stimulus something like john  and myself would say  actually this is kind of the end of the  road for a certain economic model  but in any case you know we've not  really seen this uh  happen in western democracies this  extent again since the 20s since the 30s  and by way of just how much the  ecological crisis is intensifying this  is a remarkable statistic  in three years china is using more  concrete than the united states did for  the whole of the 20th century  in three years so these things are  rapidly building up  and right now the concentrations of co2  in the atmosphere  are far higher than they've been in the  history  of primates and then some so we've been  around  been around 200 to 300 000 years but  right now concentrations of co2 probably  the highest level in several  million years so this idea that if we  adopt a sort of ethical stance take a  step back  i i think in a way that's very  irresponsible to future gene